# Store every text file as LF in the repo AND check it out as LF, whatever the platform or
# core.autocrlf says (it is on at the Git-for-Windows system level, which is what put CRLF in
# the worktree here). text=auto leaves git's binary detection alone; eol=lf is the checkout half.
#
# This is a policy declaration and not a conversion: every blob in this repo is ALREADY lf
# (`git ls-files --eol` reads i/lf for all 216 text files), so nothing is renormalised and no
# commit changes. What it ends is the worktree being half one thing and half the other — 137
# files checked out CRLF against 52 LF — which is invisible until a tool reads the bytes and
# then costs an afternoon.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The test fonts and the visual baselines are binary and are rewritten wholesale, never
# line-edited. Git already detects all of them; declaring it means a new one is covered on the
# day it lands rather than on the day the detection happens to agree.
src/DIR.Lib.Tests/Fonts/* -text
src/DIR.Lib.Tests/Baselines/*.png -text
